  ('Winter28', N'ARH 344: Museum Studies', N'A practical course in museum theory and practice. Topics include collection management, exhibition design, interpretation, conservation ethics, and the evolving role of museums in society. Includes field experiences at local and regional museums.', N'Art History', N'Fine Arts', 300, 3, N'One 200-level ARH course'),
  ('Spring28', N'POL 596: Graduate Seminar in Political Theory', N'An advanced seminar examining classic and contemporary political thought. Students engage with texts from ancient, modern, and postmodern traditions while exploring questions of justice, power, freedom, and democracy.', N'Political Science', N'Arts & Sciences', 500, 3, N'Graduate standing'),
  ('Fall26', N'PSY 318: Health Psychology', N'An exploration of psychological factors in health and illness. Topics include stress and disease, health behavior change models, pain management, patient-provider communication, and psychological aspects of chronic illness. Examines evidence-based interventions for health promotion.', N'Psychology', N'Arts & Sciences', 300, 3, N'PSY 201'),
  ('Winter28', N'NUR 581: Graduate Seminar in Advanced Practice Nursing', N'An advanced seminar exploring the expanding roles of nurse practitioners, clinical nurse specialists, and nurse educators. Topics include advanced health assessment, clinical decision-making, prescriptive authority, and healthcare system leadership.', N'Nursing', N'Health Sciences', 500, 3, N'Graduate standing in nursing'),
  ('Spring27', N'EED 295: Child Development and Learning', N'A study of how children develop cognitively, socially, and emotionally from birth through adolescence. Students explore major developmental theories and their implications for classroom instruction, assessment, and creating supportive learning environments.', N'Elementary Education', N'Education', 200, 3, N'EED 101'),
  ('Winter28', N'EED 485: Student Teaching Seminar', N'A capstone seminar accompanying the student teaching experience. Students reflect on their practice, analyze student learning data, develop a professional portfolio, and engage in collaborative problem-solving around challenges encountered during their teaching placement.', N'Elementary Education', N'Education', 400, 3, N'Senior standing in EED and all methods courses'),
  ('Fall27', N'ECN 209: Intermediate Microeconomics', N'An advanced treatment of microeconomic theory using mathematical tools. Topics include consumer optimization, firm behavior, market equilibrium, game theory, information economics, and welfare analysis. Students solve optimization problems and analyze market outcomes.', N'Economics', N'Business', 200, 3, N'ECN 101 and MTH 131'),
  ('Fall27', N'BAN 386: Text Analytics and NLP for Business', N'An applied course in extracting insights from unstructured text data. Topics include text preprocessing, sentiment analysis, topic modeling, named entity recognition, and building text classification systems. Applications include customer review analysis, social media monitoring, and document categorization.', N'Business Analytics', N'Business', 300, 3, N'BAN 301 and programming experience'),
  ('Winter28', N'ECN 172: Principles of Microeconomics', N'An introduction to microeconomic theory including supply and demand, market structures, consumer choice, production costs, and the role of government intervention. Students apply economic reasoning to analyze real-world markets and policy decisions.', N'Economics', N'Business', 100, 3, NULL),
  ('Spring27', N'THR 301: Playwriting', N'A workshop course in dramatic writing. Students study dramatic structure, create original short and full-length plays, participate in peer critique, and hear their work read by student actors. Explores diverse storytelling traditions and voices.', N'Theatre', N'Fine Arts', 300, 3, N'THR 101 or ENG 211'),
  ('Fall27', N'MUS 256: Music History: Medieval to Classical', N'A survey of Western art music from the medieval period through the Classical era. Students study representative works, examine the cultural contexts of musical production, and develop skills in critical listening and musical analysis.', N'Music', N'Fine Arts', 200, 3, N'MUS 101'),
  ('Winter27', N'NUR 444: Capstone Clinical Practicum', N'An intensive preceptored clinical experience in which senior students practice in a specialty area. Students demonstrate competence in clinical judgment, interprofessional collaboration, and evidence-based practice.', N'Nursing', N'Health Sciences', 400, 3, N'Senior standing in NUR and all clinical courses'),
  ('Fall27', N'SOC 453: Senior Capstone in Sociology', N'A culminating experience in which students complete an independent research project or community-based applied project. Students present their work at the departmental symposium and produce a portfolio reflecting their sociological learning.', N'Sociology', N'Arts & Sciences', 400, 3, N'Senior standing in SOC and SOC 201'),
  ('Spring27', N'SPE 256: Behavior Support and Intervention', N'A practical course in understanding and addressing challenging behaviors in educational settings. Students learn functional behavior assessment, positive behavior interventions, de-escalation strategies, and ethical considerations in behavior management.', N'Special Education', N'Education', 200, 3, N'SPE 101'),
  ('Winter27', N'ECN 224: Econometrics', N'An introduction to statistical methods for economic analysis. Topics include regression analysis, hypothesis testing, model specification, time series analysis, and instrumental variables. Students use Stata or R to analyze economic datasets.', N'Economics', N'Business', 200, 3, N'ECN 201 and MTH 110'),
  ('Winter27', N'CHM 239: Organic Chemistry II', N'Continuation of Organic Chemistry I with emphasis on advanced reaction mechanisms, synthesis strategies, aromatic chemistry, and spectroscopic identification methods including NMR, IR, and mass spectrometry.', N'Chemistry', N'Arts & Sciences', 200, 4, N'CHM 201'),
  ('Winter27', N'JOU 128: Media and Society', N'An examination of the role of media in shaping public opinion, culture, and political discourse. Topics include media ownership, media literacy, representation, propaganda, the digital transformation of news, and the economics of the media industry.', N'Journalism', N'Communications', 100, 3, NULL),
  ('Summer27', N'CSC 289: Object-Oriented Programming', N'An in-depth study of object-oriented design principles including encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction. Students build multi-class applications in Java, learning design patterns and unit testing practices that form the foundation of modern software engineering.', N'Computer Science', N'Arts & Sciences', 200, 3, N'CSC 131'),
  ('Summer27', N'ART 349: Digital Art and New Media', N'A course exploring digital tools and technologies for artistic creation. Topics include digital imaging, video art, interactive installations, generative art, and the cultural implications of technology-based art practices.', N'Studio Art', N'Fine Arts', 300, 3, N'ART 102'),
  ('Spring27', N'CIV 221: Environmental Engineering', N'An introduction to environmental engineering principles including water and wastewater treatment, air quality, solid waste management, and environmental impact assessment. Students analyze engineering solutions for environmental protection.', N'Civil Engineering', N'Engineering', 200, 3, N'CHM 101 and MTH 242'),
  ('Summer27', N'POL 218: Comparative Politics', N'A comparative study of political systems across democratic and authoritarian regimes. Topics include regime types, electoral systems, political parties, civil society, economic development, and democratization. Case studies draw from Europe, Latin America, Africa, and Asia.', N'Political Science', N'Arts & Sciences', 200, 3, N'POL 101 or POL 102'),
  ('Spring27', N'FIN 285: Corporate Finance', N'A study of financial decision-making in corporations. Topics include capital budgeting, cost of capital, capital structure, dividend policy, working capital management, and financial forecasting. Students analyze real company cases using Excel financial models.', N'Finance', N'Business', 200, 3, N'FIN 102 and ACC 101'),
  ('Fall26', N'FIN 394: Risk Management', N'An examination of how organizations identify, measure, and manage financial risks. Topics include market risk, credit risk, operational risk, derivatives as hedging instruments, and enterprise risk management frameworks.', N'Finance', N'Business', 300, 1, N'FIN 201 and MTH 110'),
  ('Fall27', N'CIV 483: Construction Project Management', N'An advanced course in managing construction projects. Topics include estimating, scheduling (CPM), contract administration, safety management, quality control, and building information modeling (BIM).', N'Civil Engineering', N'Engineering', 400, 4, N'CIV 301'),
  ('Winter28', N'WLG 378: Advanced Spanish Conversation and Composition', N'An advanced course developing fluency in spoken and written Spanish. Students engage in debates, presentations, and essay writing on contemporary social, political, and cultural topics in the Spanish-speaking world.', N'World Languages', N'Arts & Sciences', 300, 3, N'WLG 202 or placement'),
  ('Spring27', N'BIO 323: Molecular Biology', N'Advanced study of gene structure, expression, and regulation at the molecular level. Topics include genome organization, epigenetics, RNA processing, protein folding, and CRISPR-based gene editing technologies. Students read and critique primary research literature.', N'Biology', N'Arts & Sciences', 300, 3, N'BIO 211 and CHM 201'),
  ('Spring27', N'EXS 297: Kinesiology and Biomechanics', N'An analysis of human movement using principles of physics and anatomy. Topics include joint mechanics, muscle function, gait analysis, ergonomics, and injury mechanisms. Students conduct biomechanical analyses using video and motion capture technology.', N'Exercise Science', N'Health Sciences', 200, 3, N'BIO 101 and PHY 101'),
  ('Fall26', N'BIO 236: Genetics', N'A comprehensive study of heredity and gene expression from molecular to population levels. Topics include Mendelian genetics, chromosomal inheritance, DNA replication, transcription, translation, gene regulation, and population genetics. Laboratory includes genetic crosses and DNA analysis.', N'Biology', N'Arts & Sciences', 200, 3, N'BIO 101 and BIO 102'),
  ('Winter28', N'ACC 297: Cost Accounting', N'A comprehensive study of cost accounting systems and techniques. Topics include job-order costing, process costing, activity-based costing, standard costing, and cost-volume-profit analysis. Students use accounting data to support management decision-making.', N'Accounting', N'Business', 200, 3, N'ACC 102'),
  ('Spring28', N'SOC 559: Graduate Seminar in Social Stratification', N'An advanced seminar examining theories and research on social inequality. Topics include class structure, income and wealth distribution, intergenerational mobility, intersectionality, and comparative stratification systems.', N'Sociology', N'Arts & Sciences', 500, 4, N'Graduate standing'),
  ('Winter27', N'SCM 103: Introduction to Strategic Communications', N'An overview of the strategic communications field including public relations, advertising, and integrated marketing communications. Students learn campaign planning, message development, media relations, and the role of communication in organizational strategy.', N'Strategic Communications', N'Communications', 100, 3, NULL),
  ('Winter27', N'ARH 114: Art History Survey I', N'A survey of art and architecture from prehistoric times through the medieval period. Students examine works from diverse cultures including Egyptian, Greek, Roman, Byzantine, and Islamic traditions while developing visual analysis skills.', N'Art History', N'Fine Arts', 100, 3, NULL),
  ('Fall26', N'ENG 179: English Composition II', N'A continuation of composition skills with emphasis on research-based writing. Students learn to locate, evaluate, and synthesize scholarly sources while developing longer analytical arguments. Topics include rhetorical analysis, information literacy, and documentation practices.', N'English', N'Arts & Sciences', 100, 3, N'ENG 101'),
  ('Spring27', N'MTH 312: Differential Equations', N'A study of ordinary differential equations and their applications. Topics include first-order equations, linear systems, Laplace transforms, numerical methods, and modeling of physical and biological systems. Emphasizes both analytical solutions and qualitative behavior analysis.', N'Mathematics', N'Arts & Sciences', 300, 2, N'MTH 242'),
  ('Fall26', N'SOC 365: Urban Sociology', N'A study of cities and urban life from sociological perspectives. Topics include urbanization, spatial inequality, gentrification, neighborhood effects, immigration and urban diversity, community organizing, and urban policy. Includes a community-based research component.', N'Sociology', N'Arts & Sciences', 300, 3, N'SOC 201'),
  ('Winter28', N'POL 573: Graduate Seminar in Political Theory', N'An advanced seminar examining classic and contemporary political thought. Students engage with texts from ancient, modern, and postmodern traditions while exploring questions of justice, power, freedom, and democracy.', N'Political Science', N'Arts & Sciences', 500, 3, N'Graduate standing'),
  ('Winter27', N'PHY 306: Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics', N'A study of thermal physics from both macroscopic and microscopic perspectives. Topics include the laws of thermodynamics, entropy, ensembles, partition functions, quantum statistics, and phase transitions.', N'Physics', N'Arts & Sciences', 300, 3, N'PHY 221 and MTH 302'),
  ('Summer27', N'ACC 485: Advanced Accounting', N'A capstone course covering complex accounting topics including business combinations, consolidated financial statements, foreign currency transactions, government and nonprofit accounting, and partnership accounting.', N'Accounting', N'Business', 400, 3, N'ACC 212');
